00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
if you're looking for a job in new york  starting may 15th you'll be able to see  what positions will pay on a whole lot  more job postings this is thanks to a  new law that passed in january requiring  nearly all new york city companies to  disclose expected salary ranges for all  open positions this is a big deal  because of the size of new york city's  economy many of the biggest companies  have operations in new york pay  transparency exists to some degree in  several places in the u.s but new york  city would join only colorado in  requiring this information be included  in job postings i think it'll make a  difference in a really positive way for  job seekers especially women and  black people and people of color but  some businesses have voiced concern that  it will cause more harm than good so  what does this new law mean for job  seekers employees and companies how will  it impact the city's labor market and  will it be an effective way to fight pay  inequities  new york city's pay transparency law was  introduced in 2018 but no action was  taken until last december it comes at a  time  when the labor market is really tight  and when the power dynamics have already  sort of shifted to employees the law  covers nearly every employer hiring in  the city except those with fewer than  four employees and if companies don't  comply they could be fined up to 250 000  or face other civil penalties new york  city officials have told me that  basically at first they plan to not  focus as much on fines but i'm just  making sure that people comply and  understand this rule while the  commission on human rights will do some  investigating on its own most of the  time it will act following a complaint  this mandate joins another law in new  york that passed in 2017 which banned  private employers from pressing job  seekers to disclose their salary history  the first group that will benefit from  this new law  job seekers by seeing pay ranges they  can be more prepared going into salary  negotiations and only apply for jobs  that pay their desired salary this rule  will also enable existing employees to  compare their pay with those stated in  open roles many employment lawyers and  others expect existing employees to go  to their bosses and say hey i see the  salary ranges posted why am i not at the  high end of that range but this law  could be particularly significant for  women and people of color two groups who  have been historically underpaid  according to the u.s census bureau's  most recent national data women in the  u.s make 30 percent less than men and  that gap is wider for most women of  color  paris chandler is the founder and ceo of  black tech pipeline an organization that  helps black technologists get jobs at  companies across the us she says pay  transparency will help prevent  candidates from underselling themselves  in the interview process when you don't  know what to ask for it it brings on a  lot a lot of stress anxiety and imposter  syndrome because you don't know what to  value yourself at especially as someone  who's used to being underserved one  group that's pushing back against this  law is the partnership for new york city  a prominent business group that  represents over 300 major companies in  new york city it intends to push for the  city to delay implementation of the law  to the end of the year  catherine wilde the group's president  and ceo says the law would be difficult  to implement especially with so many  remote jobs and that in some industries  salary is only one component of an  employee's total compensation often like  in the financial services industry it's  a small component of total compensation  but you don't really know what the  compensation is going to be like for  performance bonuses or in the case of  the tech startup sector the equity  opportunities and in a competitive labor  market like new york city she says this  could put employers at a disadvantage  the idea that somehow you're putting out  there the maximum you will pay cuts you  off from talent and gives your  competitors an opportunity to outbid you  she added that the law would negatively  impact hospitals and small businesses  which are both experiencing staffing  shortages  new york city's mandate comes about a  year after colorado's pay transparency  law went into effect and there companies  have handled the law in different ways  some got around the disclosure  requirements by specifying in their  listings that the work can't be done in  the state others posted salaries with  ranges of one hundred thousand dollars  or more but overall the results from  colorado have been pretty positive  according to job seekers and others most  jobs now include salary information on  them the state has done a lot to sort of  educate companies on how to comply with  this  and as a result you can then look at  postings from big companies like amazon  and others  and see what a software engineering role  in in colorado might pay for example or  what a pharmacist at walmart might pay  in colorado it gives people a sense for  sort of what the jobs pay there even if  the pay might be different in someone  else's region a colorado trade group  sought an injunction against the rule in  2021 though a federal judge denied that  request employment lawyers say they  expect similar lawsuits against new  york's measure before it goes into  effect in may  over the next few months experts say  more details about the law are expected  to come out like how companies will be  expected to comply and what the  enforcement mechanism might look like i  think many people are watching new york  city's law just because it could have  ripple effects for other economies  around the country other cities and  states may follow new york city here and  put in place similar legislation
 

